[size=5][b][color=#ffb15c]Built in public, and nothing to run but JavaScript[/color][/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]No executable, no DLL, no native code.[/b] The download is the JavaScript that Vortex loads: checked against the 0.2.12 archive, it holds script files, their source maps, a licence and one icon. The programs it ever starts are ones you already have — your game, through Vortex's own launcher; Vortex's own 7-Zip; Windows' reg.exe, to read which runtimes are installed — and Microsoft's runtime installers, downloaded from Microsoft, only when you press [i]Install the missing ones[/i].[/*]
[*][b]Every line is readable:[/b] [url=https://github.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon]github.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon[/url]. [b]Every push runs the whole test suite in public[/b] on GitHub Actions, and the tests badge above is that run's live count, not a number anybody typed.[/*]
[*][b]Scanned on VirusTotal.[/b] The badge is generated from the scan and names the build it covers — today that is 0.2.8 — and turns red by itself if the result ever changes.[/*]
[/list]
[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/divider.png[/img][/center]

[size=5][b]What it is[/b][/size]

Event Horizon is a Vortex extension in two halves. [b]Curators[/b] get a workbench that sees the whole profile at once, reads what every mod needs, and packs the exact working setup into a package. [b]Players[/b] get an installer that rebuilds that setup one mod at a time, proves every archive matches, pins the curator's load order — and a Doctor that says so the moment anything drifts.

[quote][b]A Vortex collection:[/b] "Here are the mods. Install them."
[b]An Event Horizon collection:[/b] "Here is the exact state that worked. Rebuild it, and prove it matches."[/quote]

[size=4][b][color=#ffb15c]New in the 0.2 beta[/color][/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Collections live on Nexus.[/b] Press [i]Add collection[/i] on an Event Horizon collection's Nexus page and Event Horizon opens its install. When the curator publishes a new revision, you are offered the update when Vortex starts and on the collection's card, and it installs into its own profile, so the version you were playing stays one click away.[/*]
[*][b]A new look.[/b] Home is a dashboard now: the collection you are playing, how healthy it is, what your game is made of and what modding costs your disk.[/*]
[*][b]A different game version is a warning, not a wall.[/b] If the curator played on another version of the game, you are told exactly which mods care, and you decide.[/*]
[*][b]Missing runtimes, found and fixed.[/b] The Microsoft runtimes that script-extender plugins, xEdit and ENB need are checked before an install and in the Doctor, with a button that installs the missing ones.[/*]
[/list]
[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/players.jpg[/img][/center]

[center][url=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-home.jpg][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-home.jpg[/img][/url][/center]
[center][i]Home: the collection you are playing, its health, and what it is made of.[/i][/center]

[size=4][b]Installing a collection[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Your game setup is checked first.[/b] An unmanaged game, a game that was never started, a file from another store's copy, an install under Program Files — the install stops with the steps to fix it, not an hour in.[/*]
[*][b]The plan before anything changes.[/b] What you already have, what installs by itself, what needs you, what can no longer be downloaded — shown under the curator's own banner, screenshots and About page.[/*]
[*][b]One mod at a time, every archive checked.[/b] Vortex can lose files when hundreds of mods install at once, so Event Horizon installs them one by one, and matches each download to the curator's by SHA-256. The curator's installer answers are replayed; you choose once whether to watch them or let them apply.[/*]
[*][b]Proof at the end.[/b] Every installed file is checked against the curator's, and the finished install says how many.[/*]
[*][b]Interrupted? It carries on.[/b] Even after Vortex was killed, the install continues where it stopped, in the same profile. Every ending leaves a record under [b]My Collections[/b].[/*]
[/list]
[center][url=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-preview.jpg][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-preview.jpg[/img][/url][/center]

[center][url=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-done.jpg][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-done.jpg[/img][/url][/center]

[size=4][b]The load order stays the curator's[/b][/size]

The install pins the curator's order for the collection's plugins, lets LOOT place your own plugins between them, and puts the collection's plugins back in the curator's sequence. Afterwards Event Horizon keeps watching: the moment Vortex's automatic sort or a Sort click replaces that order, a notification tells you, with the fix on it.

[center][url=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-loadorder.jpg][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-loadorder.jpg[/img][/url][/center]

[size=4][b]The Collection Doctor[/b][/size]

Is this collection still what was installed? The profile, the mods, which are enabled, the files on disk, plugin order, ESL flags, mod rules and LOOT rules are each checked on their own card — and "not checked" is never shown as "fine". Every problem that can be repaired has its repair on the card, run through the same step the install used. What you add on top is yours: your own rules are counted, not flagged.

[center][url=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-doctor.jpg][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-doctor.jpg[/img][/url][/center]

If something goes wrong, open the [b]Doctor[/b] and press [b]Save logs…[/b]: it puts Vortex's and Event Horizon's logs, the install records and your script extender's log into one zip. Attach that to a bug report.

[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/divider.png[/img][/center]

[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/curators.jpg[/img][/center]

[center][url=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-curator.jpg][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-curator.jpg[/img][/url][/center]

[size=4][b]Curator Tools[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]One workbench for the whole profile.[/b] Updates, manual updates, frozen mods, missing requirements, mods others depend on, duplicates, disabled mods, plugins, and downloads nothing was ever made from — in one table. Views combine, a search box finds a mod, a requirement or a plugin by name, and the action bar offers only what applies to what you ticked.[/*]
[*][b]Requirements, read and acted on.[/b] Every mod's Nexus requirements and every plugin's masters, resolved against your pool: satisfied, installed but disabled, missing, off Nexus, DLC — each with the one action that fits.[/*]
[*][b]Make it work.[/b] One button reads the whole chain of what a mod is missing — each requirement's own requirements too — shows the plan, lets you choose where a page ships several files, then installs in dependency order. Without Nexus Premium it runs guided, one page at a time.[/*]
[*][b]Bulk update, one mod at a time,[/b] each verified against its archive afterwards. [b]Freeze[/b] a mod at a version and be told loudly if Vortex's own update button moves it anyway.[/*]
[/list]
[center][url=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-makeitwork.jpg][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-makeitwork-820.jpg[/img][/url][/center]

[size=4][b]Build[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Everything that made it work, recorded:[/b] every archive's SHA-256, the mod rules, the LOOT userlist and groups, plugin order and ESL flags, the installer answers and INI tweaks.[/*]
[*][b]Can your players still download it?[/b] The build asks Nexus about every mod and separates [i]file gone[/i] from [i]page gone[/i] — you are the one person who cannot notice, because your copy is already on disk. It warns, and you decide; nothing is bundled behind your back.[/*]
[*][b]Your collection's look:[/b] a banner, a card image, screenshots, colours, links and an About page, shown to everyone who installs it.[/*]
[*][b]Straight to Nexus:[/b] upload a finished build to a collection page of yours as a draft revision — publishing stays your click. Players who add it there get Event Horizon's install, and are offered every update you publish.[/*]
[/list]
[center][url=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-presentation.jpg][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-presentation.jpg[/img][/url][/center]

