[b]For the curious: which mod wins a file[/b]
[spoiler]When two mods ship the same file, the winner comes from the collection's mod rules, which the install applies. Measured on a 1,753-mod collection, the rules reproduce the curator's winner for 17,159 of 17,161 contested files; the other two are a Readme.txt and a FOMod/info.xml, which the game never loads.[/spoiler]

[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/divider.png[/img][/center]

[size=5][b][color=#ffb15c]Tech deep dive: vanilla Vortex vs Event Horizon[/color][/b][/size]
[i]The Vortex side is read from Vortex 2.7's own source.[/i]
[list]
[*][b]Identity.[/b] Vortex: Nexus file id plus the archive's MD5; a mod set to "latest" installs whatever is newest. [color=#ffb15c]Event Horizon:[/color] the curator's exact file, with a SHA-256 of the archive and of every file it installs.[/*]
[*][b]Installing.[/b] Vortex: up to five mods at once. [color=#ffb15c]Event Horizon:[/color] one at a time, then every one checked.[/*]
[*][b]Checking.[/b] Vortex: trusts what the installer extracted. [color=#ffb15c]Event Horizon:[/color] re-hashes every installed file against the curator's record, repairs what differs, and names what it can't.[/*]
[*][b]The curator's edits.[/b] Vortex: opt-in per mod (Replicate, binary patches). [color=#ffb15c]Event Horizon:[/color] finds every edited mod and ships it bundled (the exact folder, the same content hash on every build) or mirrored (the author's archive from Nexus plus the changed files, so the author keeps the download).[/*]
[*][b]Load order.[/b] Vortex: which plugins are enabled, plus LOOT rules; LOOT sorts on your machine. [color=#ffb15c]Event Horizon:[/color] the curator's exact order and ESL flags, pinned; LOOT only places plugins of your own.[/*]
[*][b]INI.[/b] Vortex: tweaks the curator writes by hand. [color=#ffb15c]Event Horizon:[/color] the curator's real game INI values, captured at build, minus the ones that describe their hardware.[/*]
[*][b]Game version.[/b] Vortex: warns when yours differs. [color=#ffb15c]Event Horizon:[/color] reads which versions each script-extender plugin declares, and names the mods that won't load on yours.[/*]
[*][b]Updates.[/b] Vortex: in place, in your current profile. [color=#ffb15c]Event Horizon:[/color] each revision builds its own profile; the one you play stays switchable until the new one works.[/*]
[*][b]Around the install.[/b] Vortex: installs into the game folder as it is. [color=#ffb15c]Event Horizon:[/color] before any download, checks the store build, runtimes, protected and cloud-synced folders, and the Creation Club files your plugins need; quarantines stray game files (restorable, never deleted), and keeps a receipt the Doctor diffs your setup against and heals from.[/*]
[/list]

[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/divider.png[/img][/center]

[size=5][b][color=#ffb15c]Four things to know before you install[/color][/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*][b]It replaces your mod rules and LOOT userlist for that game; it does not merge them.[/b] Everything is backed up first, and the backup reaching disk is a hard interlock — nothing is cleared without it. Merging would produce a rule set that exists on nobody else's machine, and it fails invisibly: every file verifies and the game still loads something different.[/*]
[*][b]Hardlink deployment is required.[/b] If Vortex is set to copy or symlink, the install is refused before anything changes: copying silently undoes the curator's ESL flags.[/*]
[*][b]Hands off while it installs.[/b] Deploying, sorting or switching mods in Vortex mid-install changes the result, so Event Horizon asks you — with Liberty Prime's help — to leave Vortex alone until it says it is done.[/*]
[*][b]Nexus Premium is recommended, not required.[/b] Without it, large installs run guided: one page opened at a time, one [i]Mod manager download[/i] click from you per mod. Nothing is blocked, and you are told before it starts.[/*]
[/list]
[center][url=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-handsoff.jpg][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/shot-handsoff-820.jpg[/img][/url][/center]

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Vortex 2.x.[/b] It does not run on Vortex 1.16.[/*]
[*][b]Windows, or Linux and Steam Deck through Proton.[/b] Both are supported targets: Event Horizon reads its own packages with its own ZIP reader, so nothing extra is spawned inside a Proton prefix. A 950-mod collection has been installed and verified under umu/Proton.[/*]
[*][b]A supported game:[/b] Skyrim Special Edition, Fallout 4, Fallout 3, Fallout: New Vegas or Starfield, with Steam and GOG copies both handled. Proven on a Fallout 4 collection of about 1,000 mods and a Skyrim Special Edition collection of about 1,750.[/*]
[*][b]Hardlink deployment[/b], as above. [b]Nexus Premium[/b] recommended, not required.[/*]
[/list]
[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Download the archive from the Files tab.[/*]
[*]In Vortex, open [b]Extensions[/b] and drop the zip onto the drop zone at the bottom of the list.[/*]
[*]Restart Vortex when it asks. [b]Event Horizon[/b] appears in the sidebar; there is nothing to configure.[/*]
[/list]
Vortex does not offer Event Horizon updates by itself yet: download each new version here and install it the same way.

[size=5][b]Questions[/b][/size]

[b]Does it take over ordinary Vortex collections?[/b]
[spoiler]No. Only Event Horizon collections open in Event Horizon; every other Nexus collection installs in Vortex exactly as it always did.[/spoiler]
[b]What if somebody adds an Event Horizon collection without Event Horizon?[/b]
[spoiler]They get no mods and a description saying the collection needs Event Horizon — rather than a bulk install that loses files.[/spoiler]
[b]Is it open source?[/b]
[spoiler]It is source-available: every line is public and readable, but the PolyForm Strict licence does not allow copying, changing or redistributing it without written permission. Versions 0.1.154 and earlier were MIT.[/spoiler]
[b]Something went wrong. Where do I report it?[/b]
[spoiler]Open the [b]Doctor[/b], press [b]Save logs…[/b] and post the zip on the Posts tab or in [url=https://discord.gg/JcaXUzWQqF]our Discord[/url]. A bug report with logs is worth more than an endorsement.[/spoiler]

[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon@1bbb7d6eb2220ece884a36a3a4cf159395e168ac/docs/img/divider.png[/img][/center]

[size=5][b]Credits[/b][/size]

Built by [b]DuduPhudu[/b] and [b]Bluuuk[/b].
[list]
[*][b]Vortex and the Nexus Mods team.[/b] Event Horizon is an extension: everything it does happens inside Vortex, through Vortex's own downloads, installers and deployment.[/*]
[*][b]LOOT.[/b] The place your own plugins take between the collection's is LOOT's work.[/*]
[*][b]Every mod author[/b] whose work ends up in a collection. Event Horizon downloads each mod from its author's own Nexus page.[/*]
[/list]
Source, issues, full documentation and a changelog written for the people who use it: [url=https://github.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon]github.com/ReidenXerx/Event-Horizon[/url]. Community: [url=https://discord.gg/JcaXUzWQqF]Dudu's Den on Discord[/url].

[center][i]Vortex is a black hole. Collections, rules, installer answers, load orders — they all get pulled in and never come out the same on the other side. Event Horizon is the boundary that captures everything before it crosses over.[/i][/center]
